PRIVATE REFLECTION: The Wackernagel second-position cluster is now treated as class ordered I particle > II enclitic pronoun > III enclitic *byti*. The pronoun-first notation host-мя-же-бо-еси-бы is widely recognised as a notational mirror of the same particle-first hierarchy and is being set aside for Old Russian.

Core agreement after round 2:
* discourse/conjunctional particles are leftmost, *že* is the fixed outermost particle;
* enclitic personal/reflexive pronoun мя is medial and precedes the enclitic auxiliary еси;
* еси is rightmost of the verbal tier.

Disagreement is narrowed to the internal particle sub-order and the place of бы:
* Scope / Old Russian-specific camp – Colleagues 1,2,3,4,7. бы is clause-scope irrealis/modal, not a flat local connective like бо. Inside the particle zone the sub-hierarchy is же > бы > бо, after which the pronoun and auxiliary follow: host + že + бы + бо + мя + еси, i.e. же > бы > бо > мя > еси. This accounts for the instability noted in C5 between *že bo by мя еси* and *že bo мя еси бы* and avoids forcing бы into the same local slot as бо. The classic OCS normalised forms *že bo мя еси* and *že bo by мя еси / že bo мя еси бы* are then treated as textbook normalisations of the same functional tripartition.
* Template / OCS-normalised camp – Colleagues 5,6. The particle block is kept flat že > bo > [by] and бы is the right-most modal element of the whole cluster: host-že-bo-мя-еси-бы, or schematically host-že-bo-by-мя-еси. C6 argues Wackernagel is a morphological template, not an icon of semantic scope, and that letting scope lift бы above бо over-interprets modality and creates incompatible chains.

Evidence retained:
* classic tripartition že–bo–mę–esi is repeatedly quoted;
* manuscript variation бо/že sub-order is attested, же > бо is the traditional textbook linearisation;
* pronoun precedes auxiliary, мя > еси, is consensus;
* instability in C5/C1/C3 between medial бы and final бы is noted as evidence for a scope distinction by the scope camp, and as evidence for template variation by the template camp.

My belief update: The textbook OCS citation host-же-бо-мя-еси-бы / host-же-бо-бы-мя-еси remains the most frequently quoted general form. For Old Russian proper the internally coherent surface order defended by the majority in round 2 is particle-first with the Old Russian modal-scope sub-hierarchy же > бы > бо > мя > еси, i.e. host-же-бы-бо-мя-еси. I retain the textbook final-modal variant же > бо > мя > еси > бы as the conventional OCS-normalised normalisation, but the Old Russian-specific solution же бы бо мя еси is now my preferred answer, with the бо/že manuscript variation and the pronoun-first mirror noted as background. Unresolved: whether semantic scope should override the flat particle-block template in the historical description.

